            <div xml:id="d69">

                <pb facs="0166" n="67" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg67"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.34119/652: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Weddell</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-10-10T17:00:00-04:00">October 10, 1936—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>216. From Braden.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>As contemplated in paragraph 2, my 206, October 3, 2 p.m.,<anchor n="48"
                            xml:id="d69-f1"/> Paraguayan September 11 note was not altered and
                        accordingly at plenary session yesterday afternoon it was accepted
                        officially. At this same meeting Ramírez delivered and read an amplifying
                        statement which largely was a repetition of his previous note plus a few
                        additional puerile arguments. However, the statement was courteous and
                        conciliatory in tone. It indicated that the differences which had arisen
                        between the Conference and Paraguay were of form rather than substance; it
                        hinted at some “compensation” for the road but left the door open for a
                        settlement of our present difficulties through the reglementation to be
                        drafted (see paragraph 3 (5), my 197, September 27, 2 p.m.). Press despatch
                        today from Asunción admits the same solution as possible.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Account referred to in paragraph 2 of my 194, September 19, noon, is not
                        yet concluded, but the opinion of the mediators is sufficiently crystalized
                        so that in view of the improved Paraguayan attitude, <pb facs="0167" n="68"
                            xml:id="pg68"/> we are now drafting notes to the parties which will not
                        emphasize contentious points but will request the parties “in accordance
                        with Conference’s August 20 resolution” each to appoint two military
                        officers to a reglementation commission. We are hopeful that the road
                        situation may be adjusted in this manner. Text of Conference notes will be
                        telegraphed to the Department before they are delivered.</item>
                </list>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed>
                        <list>
                            <item>[Braden]</item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Weddell</hi></item>
                        </list>
                    </signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
